 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570700 #edb/ref 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our discrete random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,5) & ={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(5) \\\\  & =0.2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570701 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570701 #edb/ref 1069 "q-149655" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570701 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570701 #edb/ref 1071 "The marginal mass functions of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(6,4).$\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570701 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1222ccd8-a96c-520b-9c55-3b7bc8a21989" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570701 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570721 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570721 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570721 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570721 #edb/ref 1071 "The partially complete joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=5$ | $Y=7$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.09$ | $0.36$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=4$ | $∗$ | $∗$ | $0.55$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $?$ | $∗$ | $1$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $P(Y=5)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570721 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38788558-45ed-5ac8-b24c-5c511e7f90ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570721 #edb/ref 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our random variables are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,5) & ={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(5) \\\\ P(X=2,Y=5) & =P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=5) \\\\ 0.09 & =0.45\\cdot P(Y=5) \\\\ P(Y=5) & =\\frac{0.09}{0.45} \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570723 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570723 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570723 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570723 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.38$ | $0.32$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.02$ | $0.28$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=1)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570723 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3efec416-c341-5cd9-8d7e-9c91c7f8fc1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570723 #edb/ref 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=1)=f(0,1)=0.32.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.7\\cdot 0.6=0.42.\\,\\times\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=0,Y=1)\\ne P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570730 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570730 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163615" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570730 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570730 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.07$ | $0.28$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.13$ | $0.52$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=0)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570730 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ad1bec0-7cb2-5524-90ce-c8dd7afd9873" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570730 #edb/ref 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=0)=f(0,0)=0.07.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0)=0.35\\cdot 0.2=0.07.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =0.28=0.35\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,0) & =0.13=0.65\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,1) & =0.52=0.65\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570732 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570732 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570732 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570732 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.11$ | $0.44$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.09$ | $0.36$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=1)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570732 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5dd8077d-1403-5bd2-b29d-34936c801dde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570732 #edb/ref 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.11$ | $0.44$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.09$ | $0.36$ | $0.45$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=1)=f(0,1)=0.11.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.55\\cdot 0.2=0.11.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & =0.44=0.55\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,1) & =0.09=0.45\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,2) & =0.36=0.45\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570753 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570753 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143436" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570753 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570753 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=3,Y=2)=P(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570753 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86447238-489f-5596-b009-12655e2594dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570753 #edb/ref 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $0.8$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.8$ | $0.2$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=3,Y=2)=f(3,2)=0.1.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.2\\cdot 0.8=0.16.\\,\\times\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=3,Y=2)\\ne P(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570775 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570775 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163603" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570775 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570775 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><t